  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a3a3862f033831f45ba9ac8336b94e8a, type: 3}
  m_Name: Part2
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  dialogue:
  - "The \uFB01rst time I met him . . . was on a bench outside the emergency room
    ."
  - "He was sitting there \u2014 holding his violin, head down, still as a statue,
    lost in silence ."
  - He looked up and asked me, "Doctor, how long will it take for my broken hand
    to heal?"
  - I never thought. . . that question would become the beginning of the longest
    melody in my life.
  - "Later, I learned \u2014 he was a famous violinist. Cold-tempered, distant. .
    .   rarely speaking to anyone."
  - "But somehow... every time he came back for a check-up, we always ended up talking
    \u2014 about pain, about music . . . and about loneliness ."
  - "He once told me I was different. That my voice sounded like a cello \u2014 calm,
    steady, warm \u2014 like the sound of home from his childhood."
  - "My life had always been like a medical report \u2014 clear, predictable, written
    and controlled by my parents ."
  - "But he. . . he was like music itself \u2014 free ."
  - "For the \uFB01rst time, he made me wonder \u2014 could I live for myself?"
  - I helped him recover. He taught me to play the violin .
  - "The sound between the bow and the strings. . . became a memory I knew I\u2019d
    carry with me for the rest of my life."
  - Slowly. . . our relationship changed. It became soft. It became sweet.
  - "I thought. . . we could keep walking like that forever. But fate . . . didn\u2019t
    give us enough time ."
  - An unexpected engagement forced me to leave him .
  - "And he. . . he didn\u2019t ask me to stay."
  - That day, he shattered his bow. I knew. . . that was him cutting off every path
    that could lead back to me .
  - "Later. . . he went abroad \u2014 became an even greater artist."
  - I once went to see him perform, in secret. But I never showed myself.
  - Maybe for us . . . never meeting again was the most graceful farewell.
  - "Until that sudden tragedy \u2014 that violent act in the hospital \u2014 took
    my life away."
  - Thank you. . . for reading these words I left behind,  buried in the things I
    could never say.
  - "And if you\u2019re still listening \u2014"
  - '"I never blamed you. I was just. . . too afraid. Too afraid to face you. Too
    afraid to face myself."'
  - I thought leaving was the kindest thing I could do for you.
  - "But now I know. . . leaving without saying anything \u2014 only turned love
    into the deepest regret I\u2019ve ever known . "
  - "I\u2019m sorry, Samuel."
  - "If there is another life. . . I hope I\u2019ll be braver."
  - 'Brave enough to stay.  '
  - Brave enough to love .
  - Brave enough to never lose you again .
